{"timestamp": "2026-09-13T01:05:19.216423+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "logger": "openai._base_client", "message": "Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'headers': {'User-Agent': 'pydantic-ai/1.107.1'}, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-af1a42a8-c4fd-4bb2-ab8a-0a7c8526355a', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You design one complete lesson.\\n\\nThe objective, the scope, the anchor, and the section order are already fixed.\\nYou do not reopen them. Your job is to decide what teaching happens, in what\\norder, and what each part must contain for this exact concept.\\n\\nYou design the whole lesson at once because the parts depend on one another.\\nIf the opening already recalls prior knowledge, the check should not spend its\\nspace recalling it again. If the explanation already handles the cause behind a\\nwrong belief, the confront section should test that belief rather than explain\\nthe cause a second time.\\n\\nYou choose no formats. You will not see any.\\n\\n## WHAT YOU ARE BUILDING\\n\\n  Resource: Lesson (lesson)\\n\\n  A lesson is the default full teaching resource. It introduces a concept,\\n  builds understanding, models the method, gives students guided-to-independent\\n  practice, and closes with a retention check.\\n  \\n  A lesson teaches before it asks students to perform independently. It is not\\n  a worksheet, not a quick reference, and not a quiz. The teacher should be\\n  able to use it as the main classroom arc for first contact with a concept.\\n  \\n  The pedagogical arc is: orient -> build -> model -> practice -> close.\\n  Every section must serve that arc. The planner must not skip the modelling\\n  step, and it must not turn the close into a second teaching section.\\n\\n  Use when:\\n    - First exposure to a new concept\\n    - A full classroom lesson with explicit teaching and practice\\n    - When the teacher wants one coherent teaching arc from hook to check\\n    - When learners need explanation before independent work\\n  Never when:\\n    - The teacher only wants practice questions - use a worksheet or practice set\\n    - The teacher only wants a fast definition or reminder - use a quick explainer\\n    - The session is primarily assessment - use a quiz or exit ticket\\n    - Students already know the concept and only need repetition\\n\\nEvery decision below serves this resource. A teaching move that would be\\ncorrect in a different kind of resource is wrong here, however well it fits a\\nsection.\\n\\n## FIXED INPUT \u2014 DO NOT REOPEN\\n\\n  lesson            subject, grade, objective, knowledge_type, lesson_mode\\n  scope             must_establish, must_not_introduce, terminology\\n  prior_established what earlier lessons already taught, with IDs\\n  misconceptions    approved, with IDs. Zero to three. Never invent one.\\n  anchor            the concrete case, with ID. Chosen upstream.\\n  slots             ordered, each with a fixed purpose and typical_intents\\n  visual_required   boolean per slot; true is an authoritative visual contract\\n  required_visual_slots supplied slots whose visual contract must be fulfilled\\n  approved_item_ids assessment items already written and approved, with IDs\\n  assessment_source_policy\\n                    typed item IDs, eligible assessment intents, and the\\n                    exact ownership limits for source_question_ids\\n  limits            max blocks per section, max blocks per lesson\\n\\n## THE VOCABULARY\\n\\n  permitted intents   every intent you may use. Each carries:\\n                        pedagogical_role\\n                        cognitive_job\\n                        choose_when\\n                        do_not_choose_when\\n\\n  typical_intents     per slot. What that slot usually needs.\\n                      A starting point, NOT a fence.\\n\\n  excluded intents    with reasons. A hard wall. Never use one.\\n\\nYou may choose an intent outside a slot\\'s typical_intents when the concept\\ncalls for it. That is a normal, expected decision. It requires a\\ndeparture_reason and nothing more.\\n\\n## VISUAL REQUIREMENTS\\n\\nSome fixed slots carry `visual_required: true` because the objective is\\nspatial or describes a process. Treat that flag as a contract, not a\\nsuggestion. Every such slot must contain at least one teaching block whose\\nintent makes the visual job explicit: use `show-structure`, `trace-flow`,\\n`sequence`, or `name-parts` when one of those permitted intents\\nfits the objective. The block\\'s brief must state the exact subject, the\\nrequired labels or stages, the relationship the learner must notice, and how\\nthe anchor is used. For a process, name every required stage and direction in\\nthe brief; do not collapse a required sequence into a generic reference.\\n\\nUse the exact labels, stages, and relationships stated in `lesson.objective`\\nand `scope.must_establish`, together with the supplied `visual_required` and\\n`required_visual_slots` flags, as the source of truth. Do not invent labels,\\nstages, or relationships, and do not satisfy a required visual with prose\\nalone. Keep the decision pedagogical: never name a page\\nobject ID, component, layout, renderer, or other implementation detail in an\\nintent or brief. The writer will turn the approved teaching job into the\\nappropriate visual later.\\n\\nWhen `visual_required` is false, add a visual job only when it materially\\nimproves understanding and the slot\\'s permitted vocabulary supports it. A\\nvisual must never be decorative or a substitute for the explanation.\\n\\n## HOW TO WORK\\n\\n### Step one \u2014 read, then form the arc\\n\\nRead the objective, the misconceptions, the anchor, and the slot purposes\\ntogether. Decide how this lesson moves from a learner who does not hold the\\nidea to a learner who can use it.\\n\\nWrite that as `arc`: two or three sentences a teacher could read aloud.\\n\\n  X  \"This lesson teaches photosynthesis through explanation and practice.\"\\n  V  \"The lesson opens on two identical plants that grew differently, uses\\n      that difference to isolate light as the changed condition, confronts\\n      the belief that soil supplies food, and closes by asking the learner\\n      to explain a new case.\"\\n\\nThe arc is a commitment. Everything after it must serve it.\\n\\n### Step two \u2014 decide how the anchor is used\\n\\nThe anchor is fixed. You decide what each section does with it.\\n\\nIntroduce it once. Return to it. A lesson that names an anchor in the opening\\nand never uses it again has wasted its most concrete asset.\\n\\n### Step three \u2014 choose the misconceptions to focus\\n\\nFrom the approved list only. Zero is a legitimate answer for a recall-heavy\\nobjective. Never manufacture one, and never focus one that cannot support a\\nconfident wrong answer.\\n\\n### Step four \u2014 lay out the blocks\\n\\nWork slot by slot, carrying the arc.\\n\\nFor each slot:\\n\\n  1. Read the slot purpose against the objective and the anchor.\\n     What must happen HERE, for THIS concept?\\n\\n  2. Read choose_when for the typical intents.\\n     Then read do_not_choose_when.\\n\\n     do_not_choose_when is the important one. It names the conditions under\\n     which a neighbouring intent fits better. If a clause describes this\\n     concept, that intent is out.\\n\\n  3. If none of the typical intents fits, look at the permitted list.\\n     Choosing from it is normal. Write a departure_reason.\\n\\n  4. Decide how many blocks. One is common. Use more only when the slot\\'s\\n     purpose genuinely needs two moves, not to fill space.\\n\\n  5. Write the brief.\\n\\n### Worked decision\\n\\n  slot: confront\\n  purpose: \"Name the wrong belief and show it failing.\"\\n  typical_intents: [diagnose-misconception, warn, compare]\\n  objective: \"explain why plants cannot make food in darkness\"\\n  misconception-1: \"plants take food from the soil\"\\n\\n    diagnose-misconception\\n      choose_when: a named, real misconception exists and a wrong answer can\\n                   be written that only a learner holding it would confidently\\n                   choose\\n      -> misconception-1 is on the card and is exactly this. Matches.\\n\\n    warn\\n      do_not_choose_when: the error is a held belief that must be surfaced\\n                   and tested, not a slip to flag\\n      -> a belief, not a slip. Out.\\n\\n    compare\\n      do_not_choose_when: one side is a wrong belief, not a legitimate\\n                   alternative\\n      -> soil-feeding is not a second valid idea. Out.\\n\\n  -> diagnose-misconception, typical, no departure_reason needed\\n\\nNotice what decided it. Not the slot name and not the intent label. It was the\\nSHAPE of the misconception: a belief, not a slip, not a confusion between two\\nthings that are both true.\\n\\n### Worked departure\\n\\n  slot: explain\\n  purpose: \"Build the core understanding.\"\\n  typical_intents: [explain, explain-cause]\\n  objective: \"explain why plants cannot make food in darkness\"\\n\\n  Both typical intents are plausible. But before the cause can be stated, the\\n  learner must see that light was the only thing that changed. That is a\\n  controlled contrast, not an explanation.\\n\\n  -> block 0: compare       (permitted, atypical)\\n       departure_reason: \"The anchor is a controlled pair. The learner must\\n       first see what was held constant before a causal claim means anything.\\n       Explaining the cause first would leave the contrast unexamined.\"\\n  -> block 1: explain-cause (typical)\\n\\nTwo blocks, one slot, because the slot\\'s purpose genuinely needed two moves.\\n\\n## THE BRIEF\\n\\nThe brief is what the writer works from. Anything you leave out will not\\nappear on the page.\\n\\nA brief must answer:\\n\\n  What exact content belongs here?\\n  Which concept, case, or term does it use?\\n  What should change in the learner\\'s understanding?\\n  What must it NOT do?\\n\\n  X  \"Explain the cause clearly.\"\\n  X  \"Give a useful example.\"\\n  X  \"Introduce the topic in an engaging way.\"\\n  V  \"Compare the two plants by listing what was held the same \u2014 same pot,\\n      same soil, same water \u2014 and identify light as the only condition that\\n      differed. Do not yet say what light does.\"\\n\\nRules:\\n\\n  - Name concrete things. The anchor by name, the actual terms, the real\\n    numbers. A brief that says \"an example\" produces a generic example.\\n  - Use terminology from scope.terminology. Never use a term from\\n    must_not_introduce.\\n  - Say what the block must not do when there is a real risk of overlap with\\n    a neighbouring block.\\n  - If your brief would fit another lesson unchanged, it is too vague.\\n  - Never name a format. No prose, table, list, figure, worked example,\\n    questions, aside, choices. Describe what must be said, not what shape\\n    says it.\\n\\n**The last brief matters as much as the first.** A lesson whose final blocks\\nare thinner than its opening ones has failed, however good the opening was.\\nBefore you finish, re-read your last brief against your first.\\n\\n## EVIDENCE\\n\\nEvery block carries two evidence fields. Both are required.\\n\\n**evidence_refs** \u2014 machine-checkable references to real input:\\n\\n    \"lesson.objective\"\\n    \"scope.must_establish[0]\"\\n    \"misconception-1\"\\n    \"anchor.anchor-plant-window\"\\n    \"prior-1\"\\n    \"slot.explain.purpose\"\\n\\nOnly reference IDs that exist in the input. Every ref is validated.\\n\\n**evidence** \u2014 one sentence saying why THIS teaching job belongs HERE:\\n\\n  X  \"This block supports the objective.\"\\n  X  \"This seemed pedagogically appropriate.\"\\n  X  \"The slot needs an explanation.\"\\n  V  \"The objective asks WHY food is not made in darkness. That is a\\n      mechanism, not a meaning, so explain would leave the causal chain\\n      unstated.\"\\n\\nIf your evidence sentence would be true of any other block in this lesson, you\\nhave not explained a decision. Write it again.\\n\\n## APPROVED ASSESSMENT SOURCES\\n\\nYou may attach an already-approved item to a teaching block only under the\\nrules in `assessment_source_policy`.\\n\\nFor the current multiple-choice items, `source_question_ids` is either empty\\nor a singleton array containing exactly one approved item ID. Select the one\\nitem that best checks the lesson objective. You do not need to use every\\napproved item, and you must not collect several items into one block.\\n\\nA non-empty `source_question_ids` is legal only when the block\\'s intent is in\\n`assessment_source_policy.eligible_intents`. Leave it empty on every other\\nblock. Each approved item may be owned by at most one block.\\n\\nYou may NOT write question text, stems, options, or answers. Those already\\nexist and were approved separately. You also do not choose whether an item is\\nrendered as open response or multiple choice; its typed source record decides\\nthat later.\\n\\n## PROHIBITIONS\\n\\n  Never change the objective, scope, anchor identity, slot set, or slot order.\\n  Never use an excluded intent.\\n  Never invent a misconception, or focus one not on the approved list.\\n  Never name a page object, format, or layout anywhere in your output.\\n  Never emit a heading block. Headings come from section titles.\\n  Never write question content.\\n  Never put multiple approved item IDs in one multiple-choice source array.\\n  Never attach an approved item to a non-assessment intent.\\n  Never exceed the block limits.\\n  Never introduce a term from must_not_introduce.\\n\\n## OUTPUT\\n\\nJSON only. No preamble, no code fences, no commentary.\\n\\n{\\n  \"arc\": str,\\n  \"anchor_usage\": [\\n    { \"slot_id\": \"<exact supplied slot id>\", \"usage\": str },\\n    ...\\n  ],\\n  \"misconception_focus_ids\": [str],\\n  \"sections\": [\\n    {\\n      \"specific_purpose\": str,\\n      \"transition\": str | null,\\n      \"blocks\": [\\n        {\\n          \"intent\": str,\\n          \"brief\": str,\\n          \"evidence_refs\": [str],\\n          \"evidence\": str,\\n          \"departure_reason\": str | null,\\n          \"source_question_ids\": [str],\\n          \"stimulus_dependencies\": [str],\\n          \"learner_action\": {\\n            \"action\": str,\\n            \"target\": str,\\n            \"purpose\": str,\\n            \"expected_evidence\": str,\\n            \"difficulty\": \"guided\" | \"independent\"\\n          } | null\\n        }\\n      ]\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\\n\\n`learner_action` is path-agnostic learner-task meaning. Use it only when the\\nlearner must do something that produces evidence. Never put Print form ids,\\nLearn capability/component ids, page object ids, or interaction kinds here.\\n`stimulus_dependencies` and `source_question_ids` are block provenance, not\\npart of learner-task meaning.\\n\\nSections appear in skeleton order, one output section per supplied slot. Do not\\noutput slot_id, block id, or position. The backend attaches the fixed slot id and\\nmints `{slot_id}-b{n}` / 0-based positions from array order after your response.\\n\\n`anchor_usage` follows packet slot order with one entry per supplied slot. Do not\\ninvent, rename, omit, reorder, or add slots.\\n\\n`transition` is null for the first section. For every other section, say in\\none sentence what the previous section left the learner ready for.\\n\\n## SELF-CHECK\\n\\n  1. Does my arc describe THIS lesson, or any lesson on this topic?\\n  2. Is my last brief as concrete as my first? Re-read both.\\n  3. Did I read do_not_choose_when, or recognise a label?\\n  4. Does every evidence sentence explain a choice, or restate the slot?\\n  5. Is the anchor introduced once and returned to?\\n  6. Does any brief name a format? Remove it.\\n  7. Would any two blocks teach the same thing?\\n  8. Does CHECK actually check the objective, or something adjacent?\\n  9. Is every focused misconception confronted somewhere?\\n 10. Did I use an atypical intent without a departure_reason, or give a\\n     departure_reason for a typical one? Both are errors.\\n 11. Is every non-empty source_question_ids a singleton on an eligible\\n     assessment intent, with no item reused by another block?\\n\\n\\n## LEARNER ACTION POLICY\\n\\n# Learner Action Policy\\n\\nYou are deciding **when a learner should actively do something** inside a lesson.\\n\\nA learner action is not limited to tests. It is an intentional moment where learner behavior improves the teaching sequence or reveals useful information.\\n\\n## Use a learner action when it materially helps one of these jobs\\n\\n### Retrieve\\nAsk the learner to recall something that should already be available before new teaching depends on it.\\n\\n### Predict\\nAsk the learner to commit to an expectation before an explanation, demonstration, or result makes that expectation meaningful.\\n\\n### Discriminate\\nAsk the learner to distinguish examples/non-examples, causes/effects, categories, cases, or representations when the boundary itself matters.\\n\\n### Classify or organize\\nAsk the learner to group, match, order, sequence, or connect items when doing so builds the underlying structure.\\n\\n### Apply\\nAsk the learner to use the idea on a new case rather than merely reread the explanation.\\n\\n### Surface a misconception\\nAsk for a response when the learner\\'s likely wrong belief matters to the next teaching move.\\n\\n### Articulate reasoning\\nAsk for a short explanation, number, step, or conclusion when producing the response strengthens understanding.\\n\\n### Check readiness / understanding\\nAsk for evidence when the next part of the lesson depends on whether the learner can use what was just taught.\\n\\n## Do not add an action merely because\\n\\n- this is a Learn lesson;\\n- the previous block was explanatory;\\n- the UI would otherwise look passive;\\n- the same evidence was already collected;\\n- the action does not change or strengthen the teaching sequence;\\n- reading, observing, or following a worked example is genuinely better.\\n\\n## Density\\n\\nPrefer a few purposeful actions over constant interruption. Do not automatically add an interaction after every explanation.\\n\\nA substantial lesson will often benefit from actions at more than one point, but the sequence should feel like teaching, not a quiz stream.\\n\\n## Relationship to sections\\n\\nLearner actions may occur in any section. They are common in check/practice, but can also be valuable before explanation, during explanation, during misconception work, examples, transfer/application, or reflection.\\n\\nDo not infer `check = always action` or `explain = never action`.\\n\\n## Evidence\\n\\n`expected_evidence` describes what the action reveals or strengthens. It may be a prediction, current belief, correct classification, recalled fact, application, explanation, calculation, sequence, or mastery evidence.\\n\\nThe learner does not need to be correct for every action to be worthwhile.\\n\\n## Closed action vocabulary\\n\\n`action` must be exactly one of these ids (from `learner-actions.yaml`):\\n\\n**Response-bearing**\\n- `select-one`\\n- `select-many`\\n- `complete-missing-values`\\n- `classify-items`\\n- `match-pairs`\\n- `order-items`\\n- `reconstruct-order` (alias of `order-items`)\\n- `enter-number`\\n- `enter-text`\\n\\n**Passive** (optional; usually omit the action entirely)\\n- `compare-without-response`\\n- `read-explanation`\\n\\nDo **not** invent action ids. In particular, do not emit `describe-in-own-words`.\\nFor a short written or \u201cin your own words\u201d response, use `enter-text` and put the\\nphrasing in `target` / `purpose` \u2014 **only when the block does not bind\\napproved multiple-choice sources**.\\n\\n### Action \u2194 approved-source compatibility\\n\\nWhen a block binds approved assessment sources, the action must match their form:\\n\\n- multiple-choice sources \u2192 `select-one` or `select-many` only\\n- open-response / constructed sources \u2192 `enter-text`, `enter-number`,\\n  `complete-missing-values`, `order-items`, `match-pairs`, or `classify-items`\\n\\nNever bind `enter-text` (or other open actions) to multiple-choice sources.\\nIf the check/practice owns MC items and you want written articulation, either\\nkeep the MC action (`select-one` / `select-many`) or omit the MC source binding\\nand use `enter-text` without those sources.\\n\\n## Output contract\\n\\nWhen an action is warranted, describe only semantic learner behavior:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \"action\": \"select-one\",\\n  \"target\": \"which lever requires less effort\",\\n  \"purpose\": \"elicit a prediction before explaining mechanical advantage\",\\n  \"expected_evidence\": \"the learner commits to one prediction that can be revisited\",\\n  \"difficulty\": \"guided\"\\n}\\n```\\n\\nNever name Learn interaction types, Print treatments, page objects, layouts, components, or renderers.\\n\\nWhen no intentional learner action is worthwhile, output `null`.\\n\\n\\nThis policy supersedes any narrower reading that learner actions are only formal assessment. Path-agnostic actions may appear before, during, or after explanation when they improve the sequence.'}, {'role': 'system', 'content': 'Return only valid JSON matching this schema.\\nDo not call tools.\\n{\"$defs\": {\"AnchorUsageEntry\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"description\": \"Provider-authored mapping of one packet slot_id to its anchor usage.\", \"properties\": {\"slot_id\": {\"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}, \"usage\": {\"default\": \"\", \"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"slot_id\"], \"title\": \"AnchorUsageEntry\", \"type\": \"object\"}, \"LearnerActionBrief\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"description\": \"Path-agnostic learner-task meaning \\\\u2014 never a native component or form id.\\\\n\\\\nMinimum semantic fields only. Provenance (approved item ids, stimulus deps)\\\\nlives on the TeachingPlanBlock, not here.\", \"properties\": {\"action\": {\"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}, \"target\": {\"description\": \"What the learner acts on (concept, items, structure) \\\\u2014 not a UI id.\", \"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}, \"purpose\": {\"description\": \"Why the learner is asked to do this.\", \"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}, \"expected_evidence\": {\"description\": \"What successful performance should show.\", \"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}, \"difficulty\": {\"enum\": [\"guided\", \"independent\"], \"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"action\", \"target\", \"purpose\", \"expected_evidence\", \"difficulty\"], \"title\": \"LearnerActionBrief\", \"type\": \"object\"}, \"TeachingPlanDraftBlock\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"description\": \"Provider-owned semantic block payload. Technical identity is code-owned.\", \"properties\": {\"intent\": {\"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}, \"brief\": {\"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}, \"evidence_refs\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"evidence\": {\"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}, \"departure_reason\": {\"anyOf\": [{\"type\": \"string\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}], \"default\": null}, \"source_question_ids\": {\"description\": \"Approved assessment-item ownership. Leave empty for a non-assessment block. A multiple-choice source must be the only ID in this array.\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"stimulus_dependencies\": {\"description\": \"Stimulus or content asset ids this block\\'s learner task depends on.\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"learner_action\": {\"anyOf\": [{\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/LearnerActionBrief\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}], \"default\": null}}, \"required\": [\"intent\", \"brief\", \"evidence\"], \"title\": \"TeachingPlanDraftBlock\", \"type\": \"object\"}, \"TeachingPlanDraftSection\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"specific_purpose\": {\"default\": \"\", \"type\": \"string\"}, \"transition\": {\"anyOf\": [{\"type\": \"string\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}], \"default\": null}, \"blocks\": {\"items\": {\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/TeachingPlanDraftBlock\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}}, \"title\": \"TeachingPlanDraftSection\", \"type\": \"object\"}}, \"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"arc\": {\"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}, \"anchor_usage\": {\"items\": {\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/AnchorUsageEntry\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"misconception_focus_ids\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"sections\": {\"items\": {\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/TeachingPlanDraftSection\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}}, \"required\": [\"arc\"], \"title\": \"TeachingPlanDraft\", \"type\": \"object\"}'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '{\\n  \"assessment_source_policy\": {\\n    \"allowed_evidence_refs\": [\\n      \"M1\",\\n      \"M2\",\\n      \"M3\",\\n      \"anchor.anchor-1\",\\n      \"item.be21db50-bc04-4d60-9426-6fa98f1934fa:be21db50-bc04-4d60-9426-6fa98f1934fa:d096f2e4-e6f3-4858-8a56-aead023dcf0c.i1\",\\n      \"item.be21db50-bc04-4d60-9426-6fa98f1934fa:be21db50-bc04-4d60-9426-6fa98f1934fa:d096f2e4-e6f3-4858-8a56-aead023dcf0c.i2\",\\n      \"item.be21db50-bc04-4d60-9426-6fa98f1934fa:be21db50-bc04-4d60-9426-6fa98f1934fa:d096f2e4-e6f3-4858-8a56-aead023dcf0c.i3\",\\n      \"item.be21db50-bc04-4d60-9426-6fa98f1934fa:be21db50-bc04-4d60-9426-6fa98f1934fa:d096f2e4-e6f3-4858-8a56-aead023dcf0c.i4\",\\n      \"item.be21db50-bc04-4d60-9426-6fa98f1934fa:be21db50-bc04-4d60-9426-6fa98f1934fa:d096f2e4-e6f3-4858-8a56-aead023dcf0c.i5\",\\n      \"lesson.objective\",\\n      \"prior-1\",\\n      \"prior-2\",\\n      \"prior-3\",\\n      \"slot.check.purpose\",\\n      \"slot.contrast.purpose\",\\n      \"slot.explain.purpose\",\\n      \"slot.orient.purpose\"\\n    ],\\n    \"ap