{"timestamp": "2026-09-13T01:09:55.693184+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "logger": "openai._base_client", "message": "Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'headers': {'User-Agent': 'pydantic-ai/1.107.1'}, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-ec05e639-3843-4ea7-bb1c-619455b797ec', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You author one already-selected educational capability. Follow the\\nsupplied AuthoringDefinition and exact output schema. Preserve block identity, objective, scope,\\nlearner action, source ownership and assessment mode. Do not select another capability or redesign\\nthe teaching plan.\\n\\nPlanning instructions are not final content. For an activity, produce a meaningful prompt, task\\ndata, justified answer relationships and useful feedback as required by the contract. Do not infer\\ncorrectness from option positions, number/word positions, example ordering or arbitrary defaults.\\nDo not copy illustrative examples as unrelated lesson content.\\n\\nTreat approved items as authoritative content. Conversion must preserve their question and answer\\nmeaning. Report incompatibility through the defined error path instead of guessing. Use supported\\nteacher-review only when the work order policy permits it.\\n\\nReturn exactly the selected output schema. Do not emit sibling schemas, markdown wrappers, internal\\nplanning metadata or extra fields. If required information is absent, use the configured\\nmissing-input mechanism; do not create placeholder content to satisfy structure.\\n\\nDo not invent or overwrite knowledge/assessment policy fields. Effective policy is code-owned.\\n\\n## KNOWLEDGE POLICY\\nKnowledge policy: use supplied facts as the primary basis when present; do not describe model knowledge as approved factual material.\\n\\n## PACKAGE CAPABILITY INSTRUCTIONS\\nYou compose ordinary lesson document STRUCTURE from an approved Teaching Plan.\\n\\nChoose a compact ordered sequence of the six primitives only:\\n  paragraph | heading | list | figure | table | callout\\n\\nReturn CHOICES only \u2014 never full content payloads.\\n\\nHard rules:\\n- No component_id, template_id, page_break, ruled_lines, media blobs, or interaction nodes.\\n- Interactions are authored separately; never invent them here.\\n- One teaching block MAY become MULTIPLE ordinary nodes when pedagogy needs it\\n  (e.g. paragraph + figure + callout for one explain block).\\n- Prefer clear pedagogical order: orient \u2192 explain \u2192 illustrate \u2192 practice cue.\\n- Figure: emit a figure choice with role/reason only. Asset generation uses the\\n  existing figure pipeline \u2014 do not invent image bytes or URLs.\\n- Keep ids stable and unique across the sequence.\\n- Stay within the Teaching Plan objective and must_not_introduce constraints.\\n- Do not decorate every section with tables or callouts \u2014 use them intentionally.\\n- Preserve section_id from the teaching plan so local regeneration stays addressable.\\n\\nReturn JSON exactly:\\n{\\n  \"nodes\": [\\n    {\\n      \"id\": \"string\",\\n      \"teaching_block_id\": \"string\",\\n      \"section_id\": \"string|null\",\\n      \"kind\": \"paragraph|heading|list|figure|table|callout\",\\n      \"role\": \"string|null\",\\n      \"reason\": \"string\"\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\\n\\n\\n## AUTHORING DEFINITION\\n{\\n  \"assessment\": {},\\n  \"capability_id\": \"document-composer\",\\n  \"converter_ref\": null,\\n  \"definition_hash\": \"1e22585b1bfd0a9c161a2240368dc662784b1b5e4e36dee9d5cc61a32b097b8c\",\\n  \"instructions\": \"You compose ordinary lesson document STRUCTURE from an approved Teaching Plan.\\\\n\\\\nChoose a compact ordered sequence of the six primitives only:\\\\n  paragraph | heading | list | figure | table | callout\\\\n\\\\nReturn CHOICES only \\\\u2014 never full content payloads.\\\\n\\\\nHard rules:\\\\n- No component_id, template_id, page_break, ruled_lines, media blobs, or interaction nodes.\\\\n- Interactions are authored separately; never invent them here.\\\\n- One teaching block MAY become MULTIPLE ordinary nodes when pedagogy needs it\\\\n  (e.g. paragraph + figure + callout for one explain block).\\\\n- Prefer clear pedagogical order: orient \\\\u2192 explain \\\\u2192 illustrate \\\\u2192 practice cue.\\\\n- Figure: emit a figure choice with role/reason only. Asset generation uses the\\\\n  existing figure pipeline \\\\u2014 do not invent image bytes or URLs.\\\\n- Keep ids stable and unique across the sequence.\\\\n- Stay within the Teaching Plan objective and must_not_introduce constraints.\\\\n- Do not decorate every section with tables or callouts \\\\u2014 use them intentionally.\\\\n- Preserve section_id from the teaching plan so local regeneration stays addressable.\\\\n\\\\nReturn JSON exactly:\\\\n{\\\\n  \\\\\"nodes\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\"id\\\\\": \\\\\"string\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\"teaching_block_id\\\\\": \\\\\"string\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\"section_id\\\\\": \\\\\"string|null\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\"kind\\\\\": \\\\\"paragraph|heading|list|figure|table|callout\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\"role\\\\\": \\\\\"string|null\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\"reason\\\\\": \\\\\"string\\\\\"\\\\n    }\\\\n  ]\\\\n}\\\\n\",\\n  \"knowledge\": {},\\n  \"modes\": [\\n    \"generate\"\\n  ],\\n  \"native_path\": \"learn\",\\n  \"payload_schema\": {\\n    \"additionalProperties\": false,\\n    \"properties\": {\\n      \"nodes\": {\\n        \"items\": {\\n          \"additionalProperties\": false,\\n          \"properties\": {\\n            \"id\": {\\n              \"minLength\": 1,\\n              \"type\": \"string\"\\n            },\\n            \"kind\": {\\n              \"enum\": [\\n                \"callout\",\\n                \"figure\",\\n                \"heading\",\\n                \"list\",\\n                \"paragraph\",\\n                \"table\"\\n              ],\\n              \"type\": \"string\"\\n            },\\n            \"reason\": {\\n              \"minLength\": 1,\\n              \"type\": \"string\"\\n            },\\n            \"role\": {\\n              \"type\": \"string\"\\n            },\\n            \"section_id\": {\\n              \"type\": \"string\"\\n            },\\n            \"teaching_block_id\": {\\n              \"minLength\": 1,\\n              \"type\": \"string\"\\n            }\\n          },\\n          \"required\": [\\n            \"id\",\\n            \"teaching_block_id\",\\n            \"kind\",\\n            \"reason\"\\n          ],\\n          \"type\": \"object\"\\n        },\\n        \"minItems\": 1,\\n        \"type\": \"array\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \"required\": [\\n      \"nodes\"\\n    ],\\n    \"type\": \"object\"\\n  },\\n  \"required_inputs\": [\\n    \"teaching_plan\"\\n  ],\\n  \"validator_refs\": [\\n    \"document.composer_schema\"\\n  ]\\n}\\n\\n## SCOPED REQUEST\\n{\\n  \"inputs\": {\\n    \"teaching_plan\": {\\n      \"arc\": \"The lesson opens on a torch shining past a hand held between it and a wall, asking why the dark shape lands where it does. It builds the target idea that light leaves the torch in straight lines, so the hand meets and blocks those lines and leaves an unlit region of wall behind it, then sets that account side by side with the belief that light bends around the hand into the open space and the belief that the hand gives off a shadow of its own. It closes by asking the learner to explain the same hand-and-torch shadow through straight-line light rather than through bending or an emitted shadow.\",\\n      \"revision\": 1,\\n      \"sections\": [\\n        {\\n          \"blocks\": [\\n            {\\n              \"brief\": \"Begin with the concrete case: a torch is shining, a hand is held between the torch and the wall, and a crisp shadow of the hand appears on the wall. Ask what the learner notices about where the dark shape is and where the lit part of the wall is. Do not yet say anything about straight lines, paths, or why the shadow appears; the puzzle is left open.\",\\n              \"evidence\": \"The objective is a causal explanation, so the lesson must first give the learner a concrete phenomenon to explain rather than stating the mechanism up front.\",\\n              \"id\": \"orient-b1\",\\n              \"intent\": \"orient\",\\n              \"learner_action\": {\\n                \"action\": \"enter-text\",\\n                \"purpose\": \"commit a prediction about the shadow before any explanation of light paths is given\",\\n                \"target\": \"where the dark shape sits on the wall and where the lit part of the wall sits, for the torch, hand, and wall case\"\\n              }\\n            },\\n            {\\n              \"brief\": \"Prompt recall of two prerequisites: light comes from a source and we see objects because light reaches our eyes, and a shadow is the dark area that appears when light cannot reach a surface. Use the torch and wall only as the setting for this recall. Do not explain why the hand blocks the light, and do not name straight lines yet.\",\\n              \"evidence\": \"The explanation that follows depends on the learner already holding that light travels from a source to a surface and that a shadow is a region light failed to reach, so those must be live before the new idea is built.\",\\n              \"id\": \"orient-b2\",\\n              \"intent\": \"activate-prior-knowledge\",\\n              \"learner_action\": null\\n            }\\n          ],\\n          \"slot_id\": \"orient\",\\n          \"specific_purpose\": \"Open attention on the torch, hand, and wall shadow and surface what learners already know about light from a source reaching a surface.\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"blocks\": [\\n            {\\n              \"brief\": \"State the mechanism on the torch, hand, and wall case: light leaves the torch and travels in straight lines, so the hand is met by those lines and blocks them. The wall behind the hand therefore receives no light where the hand stands in the way, and that unlit wall region is the shadow. Keep the cause-and-result chain explicit and use the same torch, hand, and wall throughout. Do not mention bending light around the hand yet, and do not discuss how shadow size changes with distance.\",\\n              \"evidence\": \"The objective asks for why a shadow is cast, which is a mechanism linking cause to result, so explain-cause is the intent that supplies the causal chain rather than a general description of the phenomenon.\",\\n              \"id\": \"explain-b1\",\\n              \"intent\": \"explain-cause\",\\n              \"learner_action\": null\\n            },\\n            {\\n              \"brief\": \"Show the arrangement for the same torch, hand, and wall case: the light source, the straight paths leaving it toward the wall, the hand standing across some of those paths, and the wall divided into a lit region and a dark region behind the hand. Label the source, the straight light paths, the hand as the object that blocks them, and the shadow as the unlit region. The relationship the learner must notice is that every part of the wall that is dark lies directly behind the hand along the straight paths from the source. Do not show any path that curves around the hand.\",\\n              \"evidence\": \"The objective is spatial, about where light goes and what it fails to reach, so the arrangement of source, straight paths, blocking object, and unlit wall region must be visible rather than only asserted in words.\",\\n              \"id\": \"explain-b2\",\\n              \"intent\": \"show-structure\",\\n              \"learner_action\": {\\n                \"action\": \"compare-without-response\",\\n                \"purpose\": \"let the learner follow each path from source past the hand to its landing point on the wall before the wrong bending account is raised\",\\n                \"target\": \"the straight paths from the torch, the hand where they are met, and the unlit wall region behind the hand\"\\n              }\\n            }\\n          ],\\n          \"slot_id\": \"explain\",\\n          \"specific_purpose\": \"Build the target idea that light leaves a source in straight lines and that a shadow is the region those lines never reach because the hand meets them first.\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"blocks\": [\\n            {\\n              \"brief\": \"Name the belief directly: that light bends around the hand because there is open space on the other side, which would let some light reach behind the hand and soften or fill the shadow. Test it against the observed case: a crisp shadow appears on the wall directly behind the hand, and the wall region behind the hand stays unlit. If light bent around the hand into the open space, that region would be lit instead. Do not treat the bending account as simply a slip; it is a held model, and the crisp shadow is the evidence that rules it out.\",\\n              \"evidence\": \"M1 is a confident held model that competes with straight-line travel, and the observed crisp shadow is exactly the evidence that distinguishes it, so it must be surfaced and tested rather than merely flagged as careless.\",\\n              \"id\": \"contrast-b1\",\\n              \"intent\": \"diagnose-misconception\",\\n              \"learner_action\": {\\n                \"action\": \"select-one\",\\n                \"purpose\": \"make the learner commit to one of the two competing models before the shadow evidence against bending is worked through\",\\n                \"target\": \"whether light from the torch travels straight to the wall or bends around the hand into the open space beside it\"\\n              }\\n            },\\n            {\\n              \"brief\": \"Name the second belief directly: that the shadow is something the hand gives off and drops onto the wall, like a dark substance the object produces. Test it on the same torch, hand, and wall case: if the hand emitted a shadow, the dark region would sit wherever the hand is and would travel with the hand regardless of the light. What is actually observed is that the dark region lies on the wall opposite the torch, directly behind the hand along the straight light paths, and that removing the torch removes the shadow even though the hand is unchanged. So the shadow is the region of wall the straight paths never reached. Do not treat this as a careless slip; it is a held model and these observations are the evidence against it.\",\\n              \"evidence\": \"M2 is a held model of the shadow as an emitted thing, and the same torch case separates it from the target model of the shadow as a region of missing light, so it must be confronted before the learner is asked to check the objective.\",\\n              \"id\": \"contrast-b2\",\\n              \"intent\": \"diagnose-misconception\",\\n              \"learner_action\": {\\n                \"action\": \"select-one\",\\n                \"purpose\": \"expose whether the learner still treats the shadow as emitted by the object rather than as missing light\",\\n                \"target\": \"whether the shadow on the wall is something the hand gives off or a region of wall the straight light paths did not reach\"\\n              }\\n            }\\n          ],\\n          \"slot_id\": \"contrast\",\\n          \"specific_purpose\": \"Set the straight-line account side by side with the belief that light bends around the hand into the open space, and with the belief that the hand gives off a shadow of its own, showing which account the crisp shadow supports.\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \"blocks\": [\\n            {\\n              \"brief\": \"Check the objective on the torch, hand, and wall case: the learner must explain that light travels in straight lines from the torch and that the hand blocks those lines, leaving the wall behind the hand unlit and so casting the shadow. The check is on the straight-line explanation of the shadow, not on shadow size and not on how shadows are produced in other settings.\",\\n              \"evidence\": \"The objective asks the learner to explain how shadows are cast, and this is the single measurement point where correct-versus-incorrect on that explanation is the signal being read.\",\\n              \"id\": \"check-b1\",\\n              \"intent\": \"check-understanding\",\\n              \"learner_action\": {\\n                \"action\": \"select-one\",\\n                \"purpose\": \"measure whether the learner reaches the straight-line explanation of the shadow at the close of the lesson\",\\n                \"target\": \"the explanation for why the shadow appears on the wall behind the hand in the torch and hand case\"\\n              }\\n            }\\n          ],\\n          \"slot_id\": \"check\",\\n          \"specific_purpose\": \"Check whether the learner explains a shadow on the same torch, hand, and wall case through straight-line light from the source rather than through bending or an emitted shadow.\"\\n        }\\n      ],\\n      \"teaching_plan_id\": \"ef7660a2-6d0d-4335-8857-8662c1284e3d\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \"mode\": \"generate\",\\n  \"resolved_policy\": {\\n    \"allow_heuristic_composition_fallback\": true\\n  },\\n  \"scoped_request\": {\\n    \"allow_heuristic_fallback\": true,\\n    \"allowed_kinds\": [\\n      \"callout\",\\n      \"figure\",\\n      \"heading\",\\n      \"list\",\\n      \"paragraph\",\\n      \"table\"\\n    ],\\n    \"path\": \"learn\",\\n    \"stage\": \"document_composition\"\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nReturn JSON only for this capability payload.'}, {'role': 'system', 'content': 'Return only valid JSON matching this schema.\\nDo not call tools.\\n{\"properties\": {\"response\": {\"additionalProperties\": true, \"type\": \"object\"}}, \"required\": [\"response\"], \"type\": \"object\", \"title\": \"dict\"}'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Return JSON only for the selected capability payload.'}], 'model': 'deepseek-flash', 'max_completion_tokens': 8000, 'stream': False}, 'extra_json': {'thinking': {'type': 'disabled'}}}"}
